n evacuation flights will  land soon but there is still no timeline  and as jackson proskow reports the clock  is ticking  the only way out is through kabul's  airport  where desperate scenes play out as  afghans and foreign citizens rush to the  gates  to get this far they must navigate  checkpoints and roadblocks manned by  taliban fighters  who sometimes fire shots into the air  and beat people back  the u.s has warned it cannot ensure safe  passage to the airport we maintain a  watchful eye and are continuously  conducting in-depth assessments to  protect the safety of americans hundreds  of additional u.s troops are on the  ground american war planes patrol above  around 2 000 people per day are now  leaving far below the u.s goal but still  an improvement  this fragile exit strategy is all there  is  blindsided by the rapid fall of  afghanistan the u.s is stuck in an  awkward truce with the taliban it is to  the advantage actually of the taliban  not to cross the united states on this  um because there are 5 000 american  troops there  if they wanted they could probably drive  the taliban out of kabul u.s president  joe biden has suggested american troops  may have to remain beyond a self-imposed  august 31st deadline  he remains unapologetic about the  disastrous american exits the idea that  somehow there's a way to have gotten out  without chaos ensuing i don't know how  that happens  already there are signs of turbulent  days ahead as some afghans push back  against taliban rule  it's that unknown future that's driving  thousands to flee while they still can  jackson prosko global news washington  despair and anger is growing in haiti  over the slow pace of aid reaching  earthquake victims there  crowds gathered outside an airport near  lake high to watch supplies be loaded  onto trucks aid is now trickling into  haiti but tens of thousands of people  still need help more than 2100 people  died in saturday's 7.2 earthquake and  there are still fears that number may  rise  where to put polling stations still  ahead the calls to keep them out of  schools  canadians head to the polls september  20th and though more mailing ballots  than ever are expected millions of  people will still go to polling stations  to mark their ballots in person often  those stations are set up inside schools  and this year elections canada is  running into some roadblocks jamie  morocco explains  should we both swing at the same time  winnipeg father of two brent johnson  doesn't mind the possibility of a  polling station at his son's school it's  been a challenging year and a half for  schools  and this i fear this could be one more  disruption but it's only one day but on  september 20th manitoba's premier says  schools in his province are off limits  to elections canada new brunswick has  also decided school facilities won't be  used as polling stations citing the risk  of covet exposure to unvaccinated kids  under 12. ontario's top doctor expressed  his concerns too additional people  coming into that  that venue may  be a risk to the public as first  reported by global news elections canada  won't be mandating its poll workers get  vaccinated instead it says it will focus  on masks physical distancing and  sanitizing there has not been outbreaks  of covid  as a result of in-person voting but only  nova scotia which just voted two days  ago has held an election during the  delta driven fourth wave the concern  with delta right now is that we know  that it is a variant that is much more  highly transmissible  than any of the other variants we've  seen before still the president of the  canadian teachers federation sam hammond  is confident if the health and safety  protocols are strictly followed it  doesn't view polling in schools as a  problem at this time  health experts say if we minimize indoor  interactions through mail-in ballots or  alternative settings perhaps polling  stations can be you know not in the  gymnasium they can be outdoors we can  exercise the essential right to vote  while keeping kids safe jamie morocco  global news toronto  beamed up next the wedding dream that  became a virtual reality  so many couples have postponed weddings  during the pandemic or downsized the  guest list others have found ingenious  ways to bring people together a groom in  ontario who knew his bride wanted her  best friend in england to be there found  a way to make her dream a virtual  reality redmond shannon has their story  all right do we have everybody jeff  gallant and his new wife brittany  finally tied the knot this month after  some pandemic disruptions we had planned  our wedding for may 29 2021 at the royal  york hotel downtown toronto ultimately  was last august i suppose  when britney had the foresight to change  it to  niagara on the lake that set the stage  for a perfect outdoor ceremony the only  thing missing one of the bridesmaids  stuck here in the uk due to pandemic  travel restrictions so with just a week  to go to the wedding jeff secretly  searched for a surprise solution  i just went on google and i typed in  top global  hologram or holographic companies and lo  and behold art media was at the top he  was very articulate and uh and i thought  would be great on camera and so we tried  i said hey no promises but we'll try to  make it happen in this schedule that  needed sarah the bridesmaid to go to the  company's london studio in the middle of  the night ready for the big reveal so  without further ado  i'd like to introduce you  to one of britney's bridesmaids from  london  sarah reddington  ring ring  london calling oh my god  she was more or less indistinguishable  from all the other bridesmaids like my  aunts as an example they felt that they  were on star trek like like literally it  was pretty trippy though i have to say  it felt like we were  in you know  in the future  a space-age way to cross an ocean we  still would have had an amazing wedding  nonetheless of course cherry on top but  but really it was the cherry on top art  media normally charges 25 000 for a  hologram broadcast but cut the couple a  special deal for their special day not a  bad wedding present redmond shannon  global news london  very nice and that is global national  for this thursday i'm donna friesen  tonight's here canada is the big muddy  badlands in castle butte saskatchewan  we'd love to see your canada please  email it to viewers at  globalnational.com or beam it in thanks  for watching hope to see you here again  tomorrow take care of yourselves bye-bye  [Music]  you
 

